<p>
<font size=5>Planting Activities</font><br>
<b>Kurt Dostal and Denny Dawes</b><br>
<i>Wildlife Habitat Institute (WHI)</i>
<p>
Kurt Dostal, WHI Restoration Manager, started out the month of September by continuing irrigation on the newly planted herbaceous materials and willow pole cuttings.  Irrigation was necessary only during the first week of the month since wetter and cooler weather patterns developed to provide moisture. 
<p>
Kurt collected seed from beaked sedge (<i>Carex rostrata</i>), red-osier dogwood (<i>Cornus stolonifera</i>), small-fruited bulrush (<i>Scirpus microcarpus</i>), and Coville's rush (<i>Juncus covillei</i>) in preparation for the 1999 season.  He also established two additional greenline transects within the 1997 construction area as part of our effectiveness monitoring efforts.
<p>
Elk have once again made their presence known in the meadow this month, as is evidenced by a fair amount of browsing and chewing damage to newly planted willow poles.  In an attempt to alleviate this problem, Tree Guard&#153;, an animal repellent, was applied to willows poles planted this season.  
<p>
Revegetation work will end in the month of October with the planting of woody seedlings (see table below).  We decided to plant the seedlings in the fall this year to avoid the heat of the summer and additional watering demands.
